speechtext:
I am not going to discuss this question from a legal or constitutional standpoint;
I am not a lawyer, or the son of a lawyer, or the brother of a lawyer. But I believe in giving votes to women. I believe that the women of Alberta should have a vote in provincial affairs, but I believe also that they and all other women throughout the Dominion should equally have the vote in both provincial and Dominion affairs. I think they have won the right to it since this war commenced at any rate-though I was a believer in and advocate of woman suffrage prior to the war. I entirely agree with the view expressed by the hon. member for Calgary (Mr. Bennett). The sacrifices made by women throughout the length and breadth of this country since the war commenced have been rightly commented upon by the hon. member. The other day we were discussing a resolution declaring, that every one who has gone'to participate in this war as a soldier should be given the vote. I agree with that entirely, and I agree also that the women who have made such great sacrifices, who have given their sons and husbands to the country in this critical time, should have the right to participate in the solution of the problems which the country will face at the conclusion of the war. I say without hesitation that the women should be given the vote immediately. I am satisfied that in

my own province, as the result of the franchise being given to women, public life will be improved and elevated. This being true of the provinces, let us give the women the franchise in the Dominion elections, from British Columbia east to the Maritime Provinces. I have no constitutional reasons, no legal arguments to .offer. I am simply in favour of women suffrage throughout the Dominion because I believe that the women have earned the right to the franchise since the war commenced.
